# Postern Courier — live Arti onion-drop round-trip evidence — FIRST LIVE RUN
# UNAUDITED reference-prototype end to end. A passing round-trip is a PLUMBING
# proof ONLY. It is NOT an anonymity claim, NOT a security claim. The onion
# address crypto is CLASSICAL Tor (the ML-KEM-1024+X25519 hybrid protects the
# PAYLOAD, not circuit metadata). Payload size and timing remain observable
# (length-revealing AEAD, no padding). The wire frame carries no auth/replay
# protection of its own; the recipient gets those only at the payload layer
# (ML-DSA sender_auth + ReplayGuard), both equally UNAUDITED. Authenticate the
# recipient key and the sender verifying key out-of-band or an active MITM
# reads/forges everything.
#
# SCOPE OF THIS EVIDENCE: this is the first time the double-gated live onion
# round-trip was actually run and transcribed for this crate. BOTH round-trips
# have now SUCCEEDED live on this host — the bare-seal drop (RUN 1) and the
# ML-DSA-signed, verified-receive drop (RUN 2). Each published a real ephemeral
# .onion, a real Tor circuit carried a real sealed drop end-to-end, and the
# recipient recovered the original plaintext (RUN 2 additionally verified the
# sender signature FIRST, passed the replay guard, then opened). RUN 1's first
# combined invocation also transcribed an honest FAILED attempt of the signed
# variant (it timed out on its retry budget that run) — kept verbatim below,
# never trimmed, because that is the honest record and shows the network-timing
# nature of the proof.

host        : Darwin 23.6.0 x86_64 (macOS 14.8.7, MacBook Pro — dev workstation, not CI)
git_rev     : 880d415f6112bc5e68fba8b96c3a1a4c59a8feef (branch base) + this branch's courier changes
toolchain   : rustc 1.94.1 (e408947bf 2026-03-25) / cargo 1.94.1
arti        : arti-client 0.44 (in-process; NO system tor daemon is present or needed on this host)
tor_egress  : reachable — arti bootstrapped and published ephemeral onion descriptors from this network
gate        : DOUBLE-gated — #[ignore] AND POSTERN_LIVE_TOR=1; neither ever runs in CI. Diff weakens no gate.

# ── HOST BREAKAGE FOUND + FIXED (does NOT weaken any gate or claim) ──
# The very first live run (2026-07-16T04:51:51Z) did not reach Tor at all: it
# panicked in rustls before any circuit, because arti's TLS (futures-rustls)
# enables no crypto-provider feature and leaves the choice to the application,
# and nothing in this crate had installed one:
error_verbatim: Could not automatically determine the process-level CryptoProvider from Rustls crate features. Call CryptoProvider::install_default() before this point to select a provider manually, or make sure exactly one of the 'aws-lc-rs' and 'ring' features is enabled.
fix_1       : add `rustls` (features ["ring","std"]) under the `onion-transport` feature ONLY, and a
              `transport::ensure_rustls_crypto_provider()` helper (installs the ring provider,
              idempotent — first caller wins) called before the first bootstrap in both live tests
              and both example bootstrap sites. `ring` is permissive (ISC/MIT-style); no GPL, no
              network fetch of any binary. Cargo.lock gained exactly one line ("rustls" under
              postern-courier); no new package was added to the tree.
fix_2       : after rustls was fixed, the first real round-trip attempt showed the sender reporting
              "served" while the recipient received nothing — `Sender::deliver_once{,_signed}`
              returned as soon as the local write flushed, and consuming the Sender tore the whole
              onion service down, discarding in-flight cells. Added `drain_until_peer_eof` (bounded
              by DEFAULT_DROP_TIMEOUT, outcome ignored) so the service stays up until the peer
              half-closes. This is a teardown-ordering fix, NOT an ACK — peer EOF proves nothing was
              received/verified, and a hostile peer can hold the stream open (documented in code).
fix_3       : the live tests and example now do bounded runbook-style RETRIES on both sides (a single
              120 s window per side is not enough while a fresh HS descriptor propagates), and the
              example additionally retries a transient re-publish failure. No gate touched.

